window.COACH = {"page": "protein-design/methodology", "title": "Protein Design · Methodology", "prizeName": "Best Model", "summary": {"verdict": "This is the strongest methods writing in the cluster and it is being read as if it were a page. It is not: it is the method half of a four-page cluster whose results live elsewhere, and no Best Model winner splits its modelling work across subpages at all, so a judge arriving from the hub cannot tell what this page covers, how long it is, or where the numbers went. Section by section the work is good, with restraints cross-validated against an independent predictor, a failed first placement published with its repair, a literature expectation reported as refuted, and the team's own primary metric discounted on its own evidence. What holds it back is orientation at the top, a set of claims the sibling pages have already overtaken and this page still states, and a notation habit that gives docking scores, occupancies and distances as bare numbers while the cluster's own limitations admit single-seed variance.", "strengths": ["Fold labels that carry the result: the identity count, the active residue count, fnat on the threaded peptide. None of the six winners puts the finding in the heading; Peking and Heidelberg both use a bare \"Results\".", "Counts given as denominators with the window they were measured over: 10 of 23, 10 of 17, zero across residues 1 to 6, and 1 of 41 and 2 of 41 for the two WHISCY runs. Every one of these recomputes exactly from the page's own alignment table and restraint lists.", "Reproducible input definitions no winner matches: the 4.5 Angstrom heavy-atom scan across both crystallographic copies, relative SASA 0.40 by Shrake-Rupley, the 8 Angstrom neighbour rule, the published restraint lists, and the analysis window broken down nucleotide by nucleotide.", "Self-correction kept on the page: the Koller and Bent expectation reported as refuted, the intertwined BAK1 placement published with its Kabsch repair, HADDOCK score retired as evidence of correctness, and the word ensemble qualified after being used.", "The LEA14 substitution block, where every edit is tied to a failure mode in ReLEAF's own delivery route and to a bench test that could kill it."], "priorities": ["Put four or five lines of orientation at the top of section 1: what this page decides, what the three sibling pages hold, and where the numbers are. No Best Model winner uses subpages, so a judge has no precedent to lean on, and this page has no abstract sheet while Protein Design has one. One note here is worth more than three notes about sentences in the middle.", "Catch this page up with its own siblings. Sprints and Results says the conserved block stops at 20 and not 23, that the alanine scan found no penalty at the degenerate positions, that the truncation cliffs are a hypothesis and not a measurement of lost binding, and that K18R was not ordered. Molecular Dynamics says the C-terminal 6xHis tag disables the Arg487 contact. This page still states the superseded version of all five, and it is the page a judge reads first.", "Resolve the LEA14 truncation boundary. Section 2.5 cuts at residue 23 on the published structure; section 3.4 measures the boundary at 16 and says a cut at 23 removes folded core. The two sections send different constructs to cloning, and a third span, residues 2 to 19, appears in the amphipathicity paragraph.", "Write the two missing reasons and fix the internal counts. Section 3.5 opens by admitting nobody has written up why AtPP2-B11 is the docking partner; section 5 defines molecular dynamics instead of reporting it, in a paragraph copied from the page it links to. Section 6 promises five checks and delivers six, and section 6.6 promotes an anchor that section 3.3 demoted.", "Attach a spread, an n, or an explicit statement that there is none to every number used as evidence. fnat 0.974, ipTM 0.73 against 0.74, the 8.75 and 0.42 RMSF means, the hydrophobic moment of 0.37 and the GRAVY of +0.056 are all bare, and the 8.75 figure is larger than the maximum spread reported in the paragraph above it. The cluster carries one plus-or-minus sign in 18,192 words; section 3.4's mean-and-maximum paragraph already shows the fix."], "ballot": [{"question": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?", "status": "partial", "where": "1.1 Background; 3.3 Receptor restraint mapping; 6 Triage: structure scoring", "note": "The work is impressive and the triage stack states it most clearly, but scope is never declared at the top, so a judge cannot say in one sentence what this page takes in and gives out."}, {"question": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?", "status": "strong", "where": "2.5 Engineering axes; 3.5 Which part of AtPP2-B11 we dock; 7.2 SignalP 6.0", "note": "The verbs are transitive and past tense: the shortest construct that still binds, the F-box removed from the docking input, and a construct that fails SignalP being not orderable."}, {"question": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?", "status": "partial", "where": "3.3 Contact-derived restraints from the crystal structures; 6.2 CAPRI", "note": "5GR8 and 4MN8 anchor the restraints and the threaded reference pose, and the WHISCY comparison is a real independent check. Against that, no CAPRI class is printed anywhere, both DisVis runs are empty, and several claims here were already overtaken by the team's own later work."}, {"question": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?", "status": "partial", "where": "3.3 The restraint lists as run; 3.4 LEA14 conformer ensemble; 7.1 ViennaRNA codon optimisation", "note": "The restraint lists, the docking input spec and the analysis window are reuse material none of the six winners provides. The reference list undercuts it: thirteen tools and papers used in the body have no entry, two entries are cited nowhere, two versions disagree with the body, and only one citation is numbered inline."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["1 Introduction", "2 Generate: evidence and design", "3 Restraints: structure and docking prep", "4 Assembly: structural docking", "5 Flex: molecular dynamics", "6 Triage: structure scoring", "7 Codon optimisation", "References"], "winners": ["A 100 to 400 word lead: the problem, the cost of solving it at the bench, the model named, what it takes in and gives out", "A scope or assumptions declaration near the top", "Methods before results, with tool choices justified instead of listed", "Results in blocks, each block ending in a stated consequence", "A comparison against data the model did not contain", "Limitations, next steps, references"], "gap": "This page is beat three of the winners' six, expanded to 10,800 words, and it carries almost none of the others: no lead, no scope declaration, no results blocks, and limitations scattered across six places instead of gathered under a heading. That is defensible for a subpage, except that none of the six winners has subpages, so a judge has no model for reading one. The fix is not to move results here. It is to give section 1 the lead and the scope declaration the winners put at the top, and to say there that beats four to six are on Sprints and Results and on Molecular Dynamics."}, "extras": ["This page has no abstract sheet. releaf-text/protein-design__methodology.abstract.md is empty, as are the Sprints and Molecular Dynamics abstract files, while Protein Design, Math Model and Digital Twin all have one. No abstract-zone notes could be written for that reason, and the prompt asked for four to eight. The abstract sheet is the first thing a judge reads, so giving each subpage one is the cheapest orientation fix available.", "\"one of our three protectants, among Late Embryogenesis Abundant protein 14 (LEA14), 1-aminocyclopropane-1-carboxylate deaminase (ACC D)\" has no conjunction, and \"among\" puts BoPEP4 inside a group it is being listed beside. Write: \"BoPEP4 is one of our three protectants, with LEA14 and ACC D.\"", "\"After interviewing Dr. Brophy, a Stanford assistant professor working in Bioengineering to uncover mechanisms of environmental stress resilience, suggested lots of factors\" has no subject: everything between the commas describes her, so nobody is left to do the suggesting. Write: \"Dr. Brophy is a Stanford assistant professor in Bioengineering who works on the mechanisms of environmental stress resilience. Two things she said changed our design.\"", "\"returning an estimated ΔG \\Delta G ΔG and dissociation constant\" shows the symbol, its LaTeX source and the symbol again on the live page. Write \"returning an estimated ΔG and a dissociation constant\", and check the rest of the cluster for the same leak.", "The analysis window is defined twice and the two do not agree. Section 7.1 gives \"Window A = RS (28 nt) + MF001 RBS (24 nt) + A spacer (1 nt) + SamyQ Signal Peptide (93 nt) + CDS first 90 nt\", which sums to 236 nt, while the Figure 2 caption says \"from the +1 transcription start site to the 90th base of the respective downstream open reading frame\", which is 143 nt. State the total once and use one definition.", "\"the OpenMM Molecular Dynamics tool ran on Google Colab\" should be \"run on Google Colab\".", "\"On the contrary, an intrinsically disordered LEA could exert broader\" should be \"By contrast\". \"On the contrary\" contradicts a statement; you are drawing a comparison.", "\"LEA14's effects on drought/osmotic and salt-stress protection provides a measurable phenotype\" should be \"provide\". The subject is plural.", "\"its 150+ aa length\" is vague where section 3.4 is exact: the deposition carries 151 residues with no gaps. Write \"its 151 residues\".", "Brophy is \"Stanford Bioengineering assistant professor Jenn Brophy\" in section 2.1 and \"Dr. Brophy, a Stanford assistant professor working in Bioengineering\" in section 2.3. Use one form, and put her full name and the interview date in the text; 18 April currently appears only in the image's alt text.", "\"ACC D\", \"ACC Deaminase\", \"ACC deaminase\" and \"ACCD\" all appear. Pick one abbreviation after the first full definition and keep it, including in the figure captions.", "Section 2.5 has two separate subsections headed \"Truncations\" and one each headed \"Point substitutions\" and \"Point substitution\". Since the folds are labelled BoPEP4 and LEA14, make the headings \"BoPEP4 truncations\" and \"LEA14 truncations\" so the contents sidebar is readable.", "Sprints and Results names a second position-eight design, K8E: \"K8 is the residue the K8Q and K8E secretion designs modify\". This page names only K8Q, so one of the two pages is incomplete.", "\"BAK1 is the signal kinase which balances plant growth, effect, and innate immunity\" lists \"effect\", which is not a biological process. Write \"balances plant growth against innate immune signalling\".", "Section 3.3 says \"the contact scan returned 16 of the 17 resolved AtPEP1 residues\" and then \"We treated the peptide as fully active\". The published BoPEP4 list has 16 entries, so say which residue was left out and why, since Pro10 is the one missing.", "The alignment table renders as an unbroken run of digits in plain text: \"Position1234567891011121314151617181920212223\". Check how it reads on a narrow screen and to a screen reader, and give it a figure number and a caption stating the finding.", "The Protein Design hub lists five phases and merges two of yours: \"Triage & Packaging\". This page has seven numbered sections and calls the last one Codon optimisation. Make the hub's phase list match the page it links to.", "\"an issue well detected by weather data, sensors, dissimilar to the detection of living things\" joins three ideas with commas and no connective, so a reader has to guess what \"dissimilar\" attaches to. Write: \"Abiotic stress can be read straight off weather data and sensors. A living pathogen cannot.\"", "The apoplast pH disagrees with the simulations. This page estimates 5.7 to 6.3; the Molecular Dynamics page runs at pH 5.5 throughout. Reconcile the two, and move the entry out of the block of residue lists where it currently sits unexplained.", "Three pages give three HADDOCK versions. This page says 2.4 twice, Sprints and Results says \"identical HADDOCK 2.5 water refinement at 40/10/10\", and the reference list cites \"The HADDOCK2.2 web server\". All six winners name software with versions, so a judge who checks will check this first.", "The AtPP2-B11 docking bounds are loose. InterPro puts the lectin domain at 92 to 255 and the input runs 45 to 257, so the linker and two further residues are included with no comment. \"comparable across documents\" should also name UniProt and this page, since a judge cannot open internal files.", "Section 6 says \"Docking returns hundreds of models\" while section 4.1 specifies 1000 rigid-body and 200 water-refined models per run. Say \"hundreds of refined models\" or give the number.", "\"5GR8, the wwPDB crystal structure\", \"wwPDB structure 5GR8, chain A\", \"PDB structure 1XO8\" and \"the 4MN8 PDB structure, chain B\" use three different forms. Settle on \"PDB 5GR8 chain A\" throughout. The flow chart description also says \"wwPDB MolProbity\" where section 6.4 says \"MolProbity\"."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["model", "software", "protein-design", "protein-design/methodology", "protein-design/sprints", "protein-design/molecular-dynamics", "hardware/bioreactor", "hardware/photometer", "hardware/diopal", "hardware/hydroponics"], "wiki": "0a3583e, 2026-10-13", "root": "../../", "notes": [{"id": "me-001", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Computational tools can provide great insights into protein engineering whilst acting as key decision-making engines", "title": "Open with your problem", "body": "The page opens on a general statement about computational tools. None of the six Best Model winners opens with a definition of modelling; every one of them opens with the obstacle its own project hit. Your real opening is the third paragraph, where the ternary complex appears.", "after": "ReLEAF needs three protectants that a bacterium can secrete and a plant can use. Choosing them, and deciding what to change about each one, had to happen before any DNA was ordered. This page is how we made those decisions.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-ug-heidelberg-01", "model-2024-og-utoronto-01"], "excerpt_note": "Both openings name the team's own obstacle in the first two sentences and never define their method category.", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-002", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A successful protein engineering workflow requires not only utilizing computational methods", "title": "Your thesis sentence earns its place", "body": "This is the claim the whole page is built to support, and you state it in one line before naming a single tool. It also tells a judge what standard to hold the rest of the page to. Keep it, and keep it near the top.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-003", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "In the following sections, we aim to justify the process we used to choose our protectants", "title": "Say where this page sits", "body": "This paragraph lists what is coming here but never says that results, sprints and molecular dynamics sit on three other pages. No Best Model winner splits its work across subpages, so a judge has no model for reading one, and this page has no abstract sheet of its own while Protein Design does. Four or five lines of orientation at the top is worth more than any sentence-level fix in the middle.", "after": "This page covers decisions, not results. It runs from protectant selection through the docking inputs and the scoring stack to the finished DNA. Every number these methods produced is on Sprints and Results; the simulations are on Molecular Dynamics. Read this page to see why each input was built the way it was.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-ug-heidelberg-02"], "excerpt_note": "Three bullets fix the scope of a long page in about forty words: what goes in, what comes out, what is assumed.", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-004", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "strengthening evidence through the OpenMM Molecular Dynamics tool ran on Google Colab", "title": "This stack is not the one used", "body": "Your Molecular Dynamics page describes a different setup: \"All nine systems were solvated in explicit OPC water and simulated using the AMBER ff19SB force field\", on \"a single NVIDIA A100 GPU\". OpenMM and Google Colab appear nowhere on that page. \"ran on\" should also be \"run on\".", "after": "The molecular dynamics runs used [the engine, force field and hardware named on the Molecular Dynamics page].", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "me-005", "type": "cut", "anchor": "In the process of selecting protectants, we came across the BoPROPEP family tree", "title": "Start at the finding", "body": "Eight words pass before any content arrives, and \"came across\" makes a deliberate search sound accidental. BoPROPEP is also used here before it is explained.", "after": "The BoPROPEP family tree gave us our candidate PEP peptides: BoPEP4, AtPEP1 and AtPEP3.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-006", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "By understanding this evolutionary framing, it allowed us to select the most optimal family", "title": "This sentence has no subject", "body": "\"By understanding this evolutionary framing, it allowed us\" leaves \"it\" with nothing to refer to, and \"most optimal\" cannot be graded. The point underneath is good: shared ancestry predicts shared receptor use.", "after": "This evolutionary framing let us choose a family with shared ancestry, whose members tend to share the same underlying mechanism even when their triggers differ.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-007", "type": "cut", "anchor": "We therefore selected the ordered LEA14, whose defined structure provides a more suitable basis", "title": "This repeats the sentence that opened the paragraph", "body": "The paragraph already opened with \"we aimed to select an ordered protein because LEA14's defined three-dimensional structure provides a stable scaffold for protein-protein interactions involved in molecular stabilization\". The closing sentence says it again in almost the same words. Telling it twice is one of the few anti-patterns all six winners share.", "after": "[delete the sentence; end the paragraph on the molecular-shield contrast]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-009", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "This was beneficial, as both LEA14 and BoPEP4 fail to combat one of Taiwan's largest abiotic environmental stresses", "title": "Your stress set differs across pages", "body": "The Math Model page names the stresses as \"heat, soil salinity, and vapor pressure deficit\", and the word drought appears on no other ReLEAF page, while this section rests BoPEP4's selection on Taiwan drought. That page also reports a measured salinity efficacy for ACC deaminase, \"0.58 for salinity\", which sits awkwardly beside your saying twice here that salinity was never confirmed.", "after": "This was useful, because neither LEA14 nor BoPEP4 addresses heat, one of the largest abiotic stresses on Taiwan's farmland. Our Math Model page has since measured an ACC deaminase effect under salinity as well.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-010", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Trehalose was set aside despite its established role in osmotic stress tolerance", "title": "Four rejected candidates, each with a reason", "body": "Trehalose, proline and CAPE are dismissed on three different and specific grounds: no novelty, a cytotoxicity risk at accumulation, and a synthesis timeline that does not fit. A shortlist reads stronger when a judge can see what it beat, and \"set aside\", \"excluded\" and \"deprioritized\" are verbs that close a decision.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-02"], "excerpt_note": "UToronto gives a rejected class its own sentence with the reason beside it, instead of burying the loss in a clause.", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-011", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Together, truncation and alanine scanning provided a conceptual foundation for our later mutation-design workflow", "title": "Three literature folds end the same way", "body": "Tang, Yu and Pearce each close on \"informed\", \"supported\" or \"provided a conceptual foundation\", so the section reads as three summaries with no outcome. One sentence naming what each paper decided would carry the whole section.", "after": "Pearce gave us the two probes we used: the truncation ladder in section 2.5, and the alanine scan that produced our hotspot ranking.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-012", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Thus, we followed with further research into the OR-gate suggesting extending to both heat and salinity", "title": "This sentence does not say what you decided", "body": "Two participles compete for the same subject, and the OR gate is circuit design, which this page does not cover. Say what the gate decision was and point at the page that owns it.", "after": "We therefore researched two- and three-input logic gates so one circuit could respond to heat and to salinity. That design is on the Engineering page.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-013", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Results BoPEP4 alignment findings 23 positions, 10 identical to AtPEP1", "title": "Your fold labels carry the result", "body": "These labels put the answer in the heading: the identity count here, the active residue count in section 3.3, fnat on the threaded peptide later. None of the six Best Model winners does this; Peking and Heidelberg both use a bare \"Results\". A judge skimming your folds learns the findings without opening them. Do it everywhere in the cluster.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-014", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Identity across the full peptide is 10 of 23, within residues 7 to 23 it is 10 of 17", "title": "Denominators instead of percentages", "body": "Three counts, each with the window it was measured over, and a zero reported as a zero. We recomputed all three from your own alignment table and they are exact. This is the notation the whole four-page cluster should be using.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-05"], "excerpt_note": "UToronto reports its funnel as ten thousand to one hundred and forty to six, so a reader sees the yield as well as the survivors.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-015", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "BoPEP4 also conforms to the conserved PEP motif SSG-x2-G-x2-N (Wang et al., 2022)", "title": "Your Sprints page says this block stops earlier", "body": "Sprints and Results states it plainly: \"The conserved region stops at 20, not 23. We have been describing 15 to 23 as the conserved block.\" It also says \"Asn23 is not invariant in our set\", against your \"Ser15, Ser16, Gly17, Gly20 and Asn23 are all present\". Section 2.5 repeats the old boundary as \"the conserved region (15 to 23)\". One page already corrected this and the other two did not follow.", "after": "BoPEP4 conforms to the conserved PEP motif at positions 15 to 20: Ser15, Ser16, Gly17 and Gly20 are all present. Positions 21 to 23 vary across our alignment, so we no longer treat the block as running to 23. Wang et al. (2022) [add to references].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-016", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "That ranking came from the alanine scan, which identified the degenerate positions", "title": "The alanine scan found no such signal", "body": "Your Sprints page reports the opposite result: \"The alanine scan returned −1.3, −8.0, −2.9 and −3.9 at S15, S16, G17 and G20: no penalty at any of the four positions the family has never changed.\" It adds that the scan \"would not have found our own two controls\". The scan ranked hotspots, and the degenerate positions came from the motif definition.", "after": "That ranking came from the alanine scan, which identified the hotspot positions. The degenerate positions come from the motif itself, and the scan returned no penalty at any of them, which Sprints and Results reports as a limitation of the scan.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-017", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "We did not apply the same family-wide alignment strategy to LEA14 because sequence conservation alone was less informative", "title": "Three paragraphs make one point", "body": "All three say LEA14 was analysed structurally because family sequence conservation does not predict structure here. The heading already asks the question, so one paragraph can answer it.", "after": "We did not align LEA14 across its family. The LEA family spans intrinsically disordered and folded members, so similar sequence does not imply similar structure or function. We analysed LEA14 as a single folded protein instead, using its experimentally characterised structure to tie each proposed mutation to a structural hypothesis.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-018", "type": "cut", "anchor": "we made several remarkable discoveries and gained new insights", "title": "Nine words carrying no information", "body": "Both phrases promise the finding instead of giving it, and the next sentence gives it well. A judge discounts a page that praises its own results.", "after": "After running a truncation ladder with HADDOCK (see Sprints and Results), two things were clear.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-019", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The plateau told us which residues could be removed without paying an affinity cost", "title": "Your Sprints page has retired this reading", "body": "Sprints and Results says the ladder's score differences come from length and charge, \"the two quantities the audit identified as the score's actual content\", and that \"the cliffs are reported as a hypothesis about which residues matter\", not as a measurement of lost binding. It also shows one of the cuts you call part of the plateau is the worst construct you simulated: \"clamp integrity is 100.0% for 1-23, 98.5% for 7-23 and 99.9% for 15-23, but 77.1% for 9-23.\" The \"within 20 units\" figure appears on no other page either.", "after": "The ladder gave us a plateau and a cliff, which we read as a hypothesis about which residues matter. It is not a measurement of lost binding: our July audit showed the score is largely explained by length and charge, and molecular dynamics later found the 9-23 cut the least stable construct in the panel.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-020", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The Wet Lab team took this on as a testable claim and is measuring secretion for the truncated constructs", "title": "A handoff with an owner and a reason", "body": "You name who took the claim, what they are measuring, what it is measured against, and why a bioreactor cares. Most teams write that something will be tested experimentally and stop there.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-02"], "excerpt_note": "UToronto reports the wet lab sending a prediction back, and rebuilds the shortlist with the reason given.", "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "me-021", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Overall, this supports our engineering strategy of minimizing BoPEP4 while preserving its predicted receptor-binding capability", "title": "A third statement of the same conclusion", "body": "The plateau sentence and the secretion sentence have already said this, and said it more concretely. Forty words of summary after two good sentences reads as a lack of confidence in them.", "after": "[delete; end the fold on the secretion assay sentence]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-022", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "This concept came after a BLAST analysis of PEP family peptides, which made us realize that experimenting", "title": "This sentence stops in the middle", "body": "It ends at \"experimenting\" with no object and no full stop on the live page. Whatever the BLAST analysis made you realise is missing, and it is the stated reason the whole Point substitutions axis exists.", "after": "This concept came after a BLAST analysis of PEP family peptides, which showed that [what the BLAST analysis showed].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-023", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "We proved this via HADDOCK; K18 could use a tighter three point anchor to PEPR1", "title": "Two other pages retire this claim", "body": "Section 6.1 of this page says of the same tool: \"In our own validation it did not track structural accuracy.\" Sprints and Results goes further: \"Position 18 still matters; the structural claim underneath K18R does not, and it was not ordered.\" The Molecular Dynamics page agrees that \"K18R should therefore be treated as a design hypothesis to be tested\". This page still presents it as proven and as a decided construct.", "after": "HADDOCK ranked both edits as favourable within the series. K18R was intended to form a tighter anchor to PEPR1 and K8Q to remove a protease-sensitive lysine. Molecular dynamics later did not support the K18R rationale, and that construct was not ordered; Sprints and Results carries the outcome.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-024", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Based on this, we ran a tandem repeat analysis. From this, we saw a rise in HADDOCK score across repeats", "title": "A rise in the score is ambiguous", "body": "HADDOCK scores are negative and more negative is better, so \"a rise\" reads as worse to anyone who knows the tool and better to anyone who does not. Give the direction in words. The paragraph also opens twice on the same construction.", "after": "Tandem repeats improved the HADDOCK score, which became more negative as repeats were added. We also considered that a longer peptide may bind less specifically, filling other parts of the PEPR1 surface instead of the active site.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-025", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The test is a gel run with and without a reducing agent", "title": "A hypothesis made falsifiable in one line", "body": "You name the test and the observation that would kill the hypothesis, which is the band persisting. Twelve words, and a judge now knows this design can be wrong. The whole LEA14 substitution block works this way, which is why it is the strongest writing on the page.", "excerpts": ["model-2025-og-heidelberg-02"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg scores its in silico ranking against a wet lab ranking the model never saw, and prints the negative result.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-026", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Helical wheel analysis gives that segment a hydrophobic moment of about 0.37", "title": "Give the range you are comparing against", "body": "The sentence says 0.37 sits below the membrane-binding range without saying what that range is, so a reader cannot judge how far below. \"About\" also hedges a number you computed. This cluster carries one plus-or-minus sign in 18,192 words, and a bare number beside an unnamed threshold is where that costs you most.", "after": "Helical wheel analysis gives that segment a hydrophobic moment of 0.37, against the [lower] to [upper] range characteristic of membrane-binding amphipathic helices.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-027", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The published solution structure defines residues 24 to 145. The first 23 residues have no fixed conformation", "title": "Your own measurement contradicts this boundary", "body": "Section 3.4 measures the same ensemble and reports the opposite: the mobile segment ends at residue 16, Ala18 already fluctuates by 1.16 Angstrom, Glu23 is 10.9% solvent exposed, and \"a cut at 23 would remove six residues that belong to the folded core\". The two sections send different constructs to cloning. The decision is yours to make; the page needs one number in both places. A third span, residues 2 to 19, appears in the amphipathicity paragraph above.", "after": "The published solution structure defines residues 24 to 145, but our own ensemble measurement in section 3.4 puts the disordered boundary at residue 16. We truncate at [16 or 23] because [reason], and section 3.4 states the same boundary.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-029", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Thus, while 4MN8 does not contain PEPR1, its resolved BAK1 ectodomain can be used as a structural input", "title": "The paragraph makes this argument twice", "body": "The two preceding sentences already establish that BAK1 is a shared co-receptor with a conserved LRR ectodomain, which is the whole justification. This sentence restates it with a concessive clause in front.", "after": "[delete; the paragraph is complete without it]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-030", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Passive residues were defined as solvent-exposed positions (relative SASA ≥ 0.40, Shrake-Rupley)", "title": "Another team could rebuild this list", "body": "The cutoff, the algorithm, the neighbour rule, the heavy-atom scan distance and both crystallographic copies are all here, and the restraint lists themselves are published further down. None of the six winners releases input definitions at this level. This is your best answer to the fourth ballot question.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "me-031", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "since single blind predictors of this class recover roughly a third of true interface residues", "title": "This benchmark figure needs a source", "body": "Your decision not to add WHISCY-only residues rests entirely on \"roughly a third\" in benchmark conditions, and that figure is the only thing defending the choice. With no citation it reads as a convenient estimate.", "after": "since single blind predictors of this class recover roughly a third of true interface residues in published benchmarks [citation], a partial miss against a 41-residue epitope is within normal behaviour for the tool.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "me-032", "type": "praise", "anchor": "We had expected Koller and Bent (2014) to provide a second, conservation-based line of support", "title": "You publish a literature expectation that failed", "body": "You wanted a second line of evidence, read the paper in full, found it reports the opposite, and then downgraded your own restraints because of it. Reporting that the literature went against you, in the section where those restraints are being justified, is the most credible paragraph on this page.", "excerpts": ["model-2023-og-toronto-03"], "excerpt_note": "Toronto takes one of its own headline targets and shows it is probably an artefact of the method.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-033", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Before committing to full HADDOCK runs, we used DisVis to test whether each restraint set describes a geometrically possible arrangement", "title": "The missing numbers arrive five paragraphs late", "body": "The section lays out the method, the combinatorial matrix, the positive control and the decision rule, and only then says neither run produced numbers because of a WeNMR login. A judge reading in order feels misled. Say it in the first paragraph and everything after it reads as a plan fixed in advance, which is what it is.", "after": "Before committing to full HADDOCK runs we designed a DisVis check to test whether each restraint set describes a geometrically possible arrangement at all. The inputs are built and the decision rule was fixed in advance; the runs are blocked on a WeNMR login we do not have, so neither has numbers yet. The design is below, because the comparison it sets up is still the right one.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-034", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The threaded model reproduces the native AtPEP1 interface with fnat 0.974", "title": "Define fnat and give its basis", "body": "fnat carries the reference pose for the whole BoPEP4 series and is never defined on this page. The figure is also bare: one model, one reference, no spread and no statement that there is none. Your own limitations elsewhere in this cluster say single-seed differences could be variance, and the notation here does not admit that.", "after": "The threaded model reproduces the native AtPEP1 interface with an fnat of 0.974, where fnat is the fraction of the crystal's residue contacts the model recovers. This is one threaded model against one reference, so there is no spread to quote.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-035", "type": "praise", "anchor": "HADDOCK score did not reliably track structural accuracy in these runs", "title": "You discount your own primary metric", "body": "A cluster ranked fourth by score with fnat 0.000, printed, and then every cluster rescored against reference geometry instead. None of the six Best Model winners retires its own headline metric this cleanly. Keep this sentence where it is, because it is the reason section 6 exists.", "excerpts": ["model-2023-og-toronto-03"], "excerpt_note": "Toronto's limitations section shows one of its own top predictions is probably a method artefact.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-036", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Over the first ten models the mean pairwise Cα RMSD across all 151 residues is 3.94 Å", "title": "A mean and a maximum, twice over", "body": "3.94 with a maximum of 5.55 across the whole protein, then 0.66 with a maximum of 0.84 across the core, and the contrast between the two windows is the finding. The spread is reported as a property of the ensemble. This cluster carries one plus-or-minus sign in 18,192 words; this paragraph is the pattern the rest of it should copy.", "excerpts": ["model-2025-ug-peking-03"], "excerpt_note": "Peking gives a mean, a standard deviation and a coefficient of variation per axis, with the simulation length attached.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-037", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Superposing all 20 models on the core and measuring Cα RMSF gives a mean of 8.75 Å", "title": "Two different statistics, side by side", "body": "The paragraph above gives a mean pairwise RMSD of 3.94 Angstrom across all 151 residues, maximum 5.55. This one gives an RMSF of 8.75 across residues 1 to 16. The second can be larger than the first because they are different measurements over different windows: a pairwise RMSD averages every residue including the rigid core, and an RMSF after superposition measures how far one residue moves. A reader meeting them in consecutive sentences cannot tell that. Say which is which, and give residues 17 to 23, which fall outside both windows and are the stretch the truncation argument turns on.", "after": "Superposing all 20 models on the core and measuring per-residue Cα RMSF, rather than the pairwise comparison above, gives a mean of 8.75 Å across residues 1 to 16 against 0.42 Å across residues 24 to 145. Residues 17 to 23 sit between the two at [value].", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-038", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Those figures reproduce the ones already in the LEA14 design documents to the same decimal", "title": "A judge cannot open your design documents", "body": "The cross-check is real and worth keeping, but naming an unpublished internal file asks the reader to trust something they cannot see. Say what was recomputed instead.", "after": "We recomputed these figures independently of the earlier LEA14 analysis and they agree to the same decimal.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-039", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Two truncation boundaries, not yet reconciled", "title": "Pick the boundary and write the decision", "body": "Stating the conflict is better than hiding it, but this box reads as an editor's note inside a methods page. Your own ensemble measurement is the stronger evidence and it is already on this page. Decide, state the cut you are cloning, and keep one sentence on why the published boundary was not used.", "after": "We truncate LEA14 at residue 16. The published structure defines residues 24 to 145, but our RMSF measurement puts the ordered boundary at 16 and Glu23 is 10.9% solvent exposed, so a cut at 23 would remove folded core. Section 2.5 states the same boundary.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-040", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Why AtPP2-B11 is the protein LEA14 is docked against has not been written up yet", "title": "The reason for this section is missing", "body": "Everything after this line is careful work on a docking partner the page never justifies, and AtPP2-B11 appears on no other ReLEAF page either. A judge will ask why this protein before reading a single confidence value. One paragraph of your own reasoning here is worth more than any other addition to this page.", "after": "LEA14 is docked against AtPP2-B11 because [the interaction evidence, where it comes from, and what a predicted interface would let you design].", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "me-041", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Restraints and other additional inputs were taken from previous steps in the workflow", "title": "This sentence names nothing", "body": "\"Other additional inputs\" and \"previous steps\" identify neither the inputs nor the steps, and section 3.3 ends with the actual lists. Point at them or drop the sentence.", "after": "[delete, or replace with: Restraints were the PEPR1 and BoPEP4 lists given in section 3.3.]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-042", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Sampling was 1000 rigid-body, 200 semi-flexible and 200 water-refined models per run", "title": "Give the other runs this same block", "body": "This is the only complete run specification on the page, and all six winners provide one. The ternary run, the molecular dynamics runs and the AlphaFold jobs have nothing comparable. One block each, in this shape, answers the fourth ballot question.", "after": "[add the same four lines for the ternary run and for molecular dynamics: sampling or length, clustering or thermostat settings, what was held flexible, and what was held constant across the series]", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "me-043", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "This threaded approach allowed us to preserve the molecular geometry of the previously modeled ligand-receptor interaction", "title": "The paragraph above already said this", "body": "The previous paragraph says you kept the PEPR1-BoPEP4 complex as established and then introduced BAK1. This one says the same thing in longer words. Merge them and keep the assessment criteria, which are new.", "after": "The resulting ternary models were then assessed for steric clashes, interface plausibility, and consistency with the restraints that defined them.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-044", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "HADDOCK searches for a favourable arrangement under restraints we supply", "title": "This section is in a different voice", "body": "Section 4.3 is tighter and more clipped than anything around it, and a reviewer will notice. The content is sound and the independence argument is genuinely good. Put it in your own words, at the length the rest of the page runs, so you can defend every sentence of it at the jamboree.", "after": "[rewrite section 4.3 in the voice of sections 2.5 and 3.4, keeping the argument: HADDOCK searches under your restraints, a co-folding predictor builds from sequence with none, so agreement between them carries information.]", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-045", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Our July methodology audit found AlphaFold 3 returned ipTM 0.73 for a peptide published as more than 4000-fold inactive", "title": "This value sits below your Sprints floor", "body": "Sprints and Results gives the panel as \"ipTM 0.49 against 0.75 to 0.89 for the rest of the panel\", so 0.74 for BoPEP4 falls under that stated floor and the two pages cannot both hold. The argument also rests on a gap of 0.01, which cannot carry it unless the reader knows the run-to-run spread, and the same paragraph says five models per seed are available. Sprints dates the audit as 30 July and names the dead peptide; this page does neither.", "after": "Our 30 July methodology audit found AlphaFold 3 returned ipTM 0.73 for G17A, published as more than 4000-fold inactive, against [the value consistent with Sprints] for BoPEP4. Across the five models per seed the two spans were [range] and [range], so the metric does not separate them.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-046", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "We applied this to our project to understand the translation and secretion efficiency of our protectants", "title": "This describes work the link lacks", "body": "Molecular dynamics of receptor interactions does not measure translation or secretion efficiency; the ViennaRNA and SignalP work in section 7 does. The Molecular Dynamics page is about binding stability and the purification tag finding. This paragraph is also byte-identical to that page's opening paragraph, so section 5 currently adds nothing and misdescribes what it points at.", "after": "We ran molecular dynamics on [n] systems for [total] ns to test whether the docked complexes hold together and which contacts persist. One result changed a construct: the C-terminal purification tag disabled the Arg487 contact, so the tag was moved. The runs, the settings and the findings are on the Molecular Dynamics page.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "me-047", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "A model passes only if it passes all five.", "title": "Five checks, six subsections", "body": "The page says \"all five\" here, \"The five ask different questions\" on the next line, \"the other four\" in section 6.1 and \"all five checks\" in the closing box. Sections 6.1 to 6.6 list six, and the contents sidebar lists six. Mapping your five questions onto them leaves PRODIGY with no question. Either fold two subsections together or make it six everywhere, including the question list.", "after": "A model passes only if it passes all six.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-048", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "It is the only check that measures correctness directly, and it only works where a reference structure exists", "title": "Print the grade this check produced", "body": "You say every protocol change is graded against 5GR8, which is the right design and the third ballot question's best target. The grade never appears, here or as a pointer. One line giving the class your final protocol reached turns a stated intention into evidence.", "after": "It is the only check that measures correctness directly, and it only works where a reference structure exists. For us that is 5GR8, where our final protocol reaches CAPRI [class]. Every protocol change is graded against it, so any claim about a model with no reference rests on a protocol calibrated on one that has it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-049", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "it is the one that caught register errors and the energy scores accepted", "title": "A word is missing, and the anchors disagree", "body": "As printed, this reads as two things caught; you mean register errors the energy scores had accepted. The same sentence also calls 487 and 491 \"the arginine anchors\" and says both were verified, while section 3.3 measured 491 at \"roughly 5 Å, consistent with a real but secondary or water-mediated contact\" and gave the anchor as Asn23 against Arg487, Asp441 and Asn465. Arg487 is the only anchor your other two pages use. As written, a PEPR2 model fails this test by construction, since section 3.3 says 491 is charge-reversed there.", "after": "it is the one that caught register errors the energy scores had accepted. For the PEPR1 interface the documented contact we test is Arg487 against the peptide's C-terminal carboxylate, with Arg491 as a secondary contact at roughly 5 Å.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-050", "type": "praise", "anchor": "It does not show that the peptide reaches the receptor, survives the apoplast", "title": "The scope statement a long methods page needs", "body": "Four things the triage stack cannot establish, named as specifically as the things it can, and then the handoff: only the wet lab closes the gap to function. This is the paragraph that stops a judge asking whether you know what your own checks are worth. Consider lifting a version of it into the orientation block at the top.", "excerpts": ["model-2025-og-heidelberg-03"], "excerpt_note": "The same four beats: the mismatch, its causes, the conclusion that survives it, and the accuracy this model actually needs.", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-051", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "We used ViennaRNA, which assumed that reducing mRNA secondary-structure stability around the MF001 ribosome binding site", "title": "The assumption is yours, not the software's", "body": "ViennaRNA folds RNA and predicts unpaired probabilities. It assumes nothing about ribosome attachment. The premise that less structure around the RBS and start codon improves initiation is yours, taken from Castillo-Hair, and attributing it to the tool hides the one place a judge wants to see you own it.", "after": "We assumed, following Castillo-Hair et al. [5], that reducing mRNA secondary-structure stability around the MF001 ribosome binding site and the start codon improves translation initiation. We tested that with ViennaRNA, using minimum free energy values and unpaired-probability predictions.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-052", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "In order to improve the effectiveness of protein translation efficiency in the CcaSR optogenetic system", "title": "The motivation arrives after the result", "body": "Paragraph one gives the conclusion about recoding codons 2 to 31, and paragraph two then introduces ViennaRNA and the premise for the first time. Swap them so the premise, the window and the metrics come before the finding.", "after": "[move this paragraph above the one that opens \"We used ViennaRNA\", so the order runs premise, window, metrics, phases, result]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-053", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "A quantitative metric balance score was established as bal = start+15 + SD core", "title": "Say why the two terms carry equal weight", "body": "You are adding two mean probabilities and maximising the sum. A reader will ask whether the two matter equally, and whether a candidate can win on one while failing the other. One clause settles it.", "after": "We scored candidates on bal = start+15 + SD core, weighting the two accessibility terms equally because [reason]. Both are mean unpaired probabilities, so the sum runs from zero to two.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-054", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "while also appending a C-terminal 6xHis purification tag", "title": "This tag was overtaken elsewhere", "body": "Your Molecular Dynamics page found that a C-terminal tag means \"the bidentate salt bridge was missing in every simulated frame in contrast to 100% of native frames\", and your Protein Design abstract calls it a tag that \"turned out, once simulated, to disable the exact contact the peptide depends on\". This is the cluster's headline finding and this page, the only one that specifies the tag, does not mention it.", "after": "Phase 2 addressed elongation performance through CAI, using the host matrix as the standard. We also appended a C-terminal 6xHis purification tag at this stage, which molecular dynamics later showed disables the Arg487 contact; see Molecular Dynamics for the result and the tag position we settled on.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-055", "type": "praise", "anchor": "However, the full-length optimization degraded translation initiation by causing the start+15 value to decrease", "title": "A phase reported as having made things worse", "body": "The three phases are a chronology with a failure in the middle, and that failure gets its own sentence with the metric that detected it. Ordering a methods section by what changed between attempts is the most honest of the six winner structures, and this is the one place on the page that does it.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-04"], "excerpt_note": "UToronto runs the same four beats: the negative result, its diagnosis, the fix, and the limit on the fix.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "me-056", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Phase 3 deviated away from standard host codon adaptation by making the SamyQ codons 2 to 31 editable", "title": "Say the protein sequence does not change", "body": "\"Making the SamyQ codons editable\" can be read as mutating the signal peptide, which would be a serious change to a secretion construct. You mean synonymous recoding. The page says so two sentences earlier and not here, which is where it matters.", "after": "Phase 3 departed from standard host codon adaptation by allowing synonymous recoding of SamyQ codons 2 to 31. The signal peptide's amino acid sequence is unchanged; only its codons differ, which further reduces mRNA structure around the start site.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-057", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "(D) Secondary-structure analysis of SamyQ-AmilCP.", "title": "AmilCP appears only in the figures", "body": "AmilCP is one of the four cargo sequences in both figures and is never named in the body, which introduces three protectants. A judge cannot tell whether it is a fourth protectant, a reporter or a control.", "after": "[name AmilCP in the section 7.1 text at first use, with one clause saying what it is and why it is in the panel]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-058", "type": "cut", "anchor": "the rigid baseline assembly masking the RBS is bypassed during optimization", "title": "Four panel captions say one thing four ways", "body": "Captions A to D all report that the RBS baseline is largely unchanged while the early open reading frame opens up, in four different wordings, and this phrase is the least clear of them. One caption stating the shared finding, plus a line per panel for what differs, is shorter and says more.", "after": "Figure 2 | In all four cargoes the upstream RBS baseline is largely unchanged while unpaired probability rises across the start codon and the first 15 nucleotides. (A) BoPEP4. (B) LEA14. (C) ACCD. (D) AmilCP, [the one difference worth noting].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "me-059", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A construct that passes structural triage and fails this check is not orderable", "title": "A hard decision rule, stated as one", "body": "Thirteen words telling a judge the model has veto power over what gets ordered. Nothing in the second ballot question is answered better than a sentence where a computational result stops a construct.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "me-060", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "de Vries, S. J. & Bonvin, A. M. J. J. CPORT: a consensus interface predictor", "title": "The reference list and the body disagree", "body": "AlphaFold, WHISCY, CAPRI, BLAST, InterPro, SWISS-MODEL, OpenMM, Kazusa, Shrake-Rupley, Kabsch, Yu et al., Wang et al. and Alexandre Bonvin are all used in the body with no entry here, while CPORT and ePlant have entries and are cited nowhere. Only Castillo-Hair is numbered inline. All six Best Model winners cite their tools as literature and number them inline; this is the cheapest point available on the fourth ballot question.", "after": "[add an entry for every tool and paper named in the body, cite each one inline at first use, fix the HADDOCK and ViennaRNA version mismatches, and either cite CPORT and ePlant where they were used or remove them]", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}], "excerpts": {"model-2023-og-toronto-03": {"id": "model-2023-og-toronto-03", "prize": "model", "team": "Toronto", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/toronto/model", "section": "5.2 Limitations and Applications", "function": "limitation", "text": "In the second case, which is, in fact, one of the top targets for overexpression predicted by our algorithms, a small increase in fluxes through Triose-phosphate isomerase (TPI) correlates with the highest growth rates. However, as can be observed from Figure 6, this relationship is not uniformly positive throughout the entire possible flux ranges. This makes TPI a less than ideal candidate for overexpression than what FSEOF suggests.", "why_it_works": "The team takes one of its own headline overexpression targets and shows it is probably an artefact of the method. Admitting that a published result of yours is weak, in the section where a reader will look for it, is the strongest credibility move on any of these pages.", "teaches": ["uncertainty", "evidence", "voice"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-og-utoronto-01": {"id": "model-2024-og-utoronto-01", "prize": "model", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/model", "section": "Abstract", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Plasmids are essential vectors in synthetic biology, facilitating the delivery of genetic material in bacterial systems. However, the generation of novel plasmid sequences with targeted functionalities presents a significant challenge, and no experimentally validated models exist for reliably designing biologically viable, de novo plasmids. We present a novel plasmid generation pipeline that integrates machine learning with experimental validation to produce functional plasmid sequences. Utilizing a custom Byte Pair Encoding tokenizer and the Mamba2 state-space model architecture, our model efficiently handles the long and complex nature of plasmid DNA. The model was trained in two phases: pre-training on a comprehensive dataset of over 137,000 plasmid sequences, followed by fine-tuning on specific target sequences.", "why_it_works": "The gap is stated as an absence that can be checked (no experimentally validated models exist), then the claim, then the architecture, then the size of the training set. Four moves, no adjectives doing the work.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-og-utoronto-02": {"id": "model-2024-og-utoronto-02", "prize": "model", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/model", "section": "Batch 1", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "This encouraged us to dive deeper into the batch and select top sequences for in vitro validation. An initial set of 6 oris, a mix of Class A and Class B was selected. However, after attempting to design these plasmids in Wet Lab, Class A plasmids were deemed to be too difficult to test at this stage, as their machinery is more complex and difficult to detect. This Wet Lab - Dry Lab iteration meant that instead 5 promising Class B oris were delivered to Wet Lab. Finally, one promising protein was also selected. None of the oris in this batch worked.", "why_it_works": "The wet lab sends a prediction back: Class A plasmids are too hard to test, so the shortlist is rebuilt and the reason is given. Then the six-word verdict on the whole batch. The failure gets its own sentence instead of being buried in a clause.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "voice", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-og-utoronto-04": {"id": "model-2024-og-utoronto-04", "prize": "model", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/model", "section": "Key Ori Features and Classes", "function": "limitation", "text": "These negative scores suggest poor alignment between the generated sequences and the class B elements.\nThese negative scores are likely due to a lack of sequence variability in the class B elements that were used. For example, only one version of the P2 promoter and RNA II region (From E. coli ColE1 plasmid) were used, limiting the accuracy of the alignments. Using a set of variants for each sequence could improve alignment scores. However, the lack of established consequence sequences for a P2 promoter and RNA II coding region make this a difficult next step to pursue.", "why_it_works": "A negative result, a diagnosis of why it is negative (one reference sequence rather than a set), the fix that would follow, and then the admission that the fix is blocked too. Four steps, and no retreat into 'further work is needed'.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-og-utoronto-05": {"id": "model-2024-og-utoronto-05", "prize": "model", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/model", "section": "Protein Selection", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Most of the DNA sequences from batch 3 that align with the AMR database are too short to form a viable protein (less than 300mAA) when blasted for ORFs for amino acid sequence translation. Out of a batch of 10,000 sequences, 140 DNA sequences were found to have aligned with the AMRFinderPlus database above the minimum 20% coverage to reference sequences, and only 6 translated amino acid sequences were predicted to form viable 3D protein structures.\nOne good indicator of the model performance is the lack of overfitting. As seen in the percentage coverage of reference length, half of the selected predicted proteins are only ~50% in comparison to wild type.", "why_it_works": "The funnel is reported at every stage, 10,000 sequences down to 140 down to 6, so a reader sees the yield and not only the survivors. The overfitting check is then run against the model's own output and reported as a number.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-01": {"id": "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-01", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "Our Model DaVinci", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Engineering the spatial 3D genome organization by artificially inducing DNA-DNA interactions with our PICasSO “staple” proteins offers profoundly new avenues in synthetic biology and genomics. Still, trial-and-error iterations through various staple designs and experimental conditions can be labor-intensive, costly, and time-consuming. To overcome this limitation, we developed DaVinci , an in silico model for rapid engineering and prototyping of our PICasSO system. DaVinci acts as a digital twin to PICasSO and is designed to understand the forces acting on the DNA in presence of PICasSO staple proteins, refine experimental parameters and optimize the spatial connections between protein staples and target DNA. To accomplish this task, we divided the problem into three parts.", "why_it_works": "Four sentences get a judge from the biology to the reason a model exists: the experiments are slow, costly and labour-intensive, so a digital twin replaces the trial and error. The model is named and given a job before any method appears.", "teaches": ["structure", "clarity", "audience-framing"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-02": {"id": "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-02", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "DaVinci in a Nutshell", "function": "definition", "text": "- Input: staple target DNA sequence, experimental condition, and staple protein structure\n- Output stapling feasibility, staple design, and binding characteristics including binding energy and dynamics\n- Assumes only Newtonian mechanics and conventional base pair interactions", "why_it_works": "Three bullets declare what the model takes, what it returns, and what physics it assumes. The scope of a long page is fixed in about forty words, so a reader knows what not to expect from it.", "teaches": ["structure", "concision", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2025-og-heidelberg-02": {"id": "model-2025-og-heidelberg-02", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "Results: Wet lab binder and metric validation", "function": "validation", "text": "The ranking of the experimentally validated binders was subsequently compared to BindCraft-derived i_pTM scores, as well as KD-based rankings acquired through our US simulations and Prodigy. For this, the Kendall's τ as well as Spearman correlation metric were utilized, whereas Kendall's τ is better suited for quantifying ranking similarity for the small sample size of n = 5 binders (Kendall, 1938). We found that both the US-derived ranking showed the better alignment with the experimental data than the BindCraft i_pTM ranking, with a Kendall's τUS = 0.80 compared to τBindCraft = −0.20 (Table 4), whereas Prodigy also performed relatively well with a Kendall's τProdigy = 0.40.", "why_it_works": "The in silico rankings are scored against a wet lab ranking the model never saw, and the statistic is chosen because the sample is small (n = 5) rather than because it flatters. The team's own generator scores negative, and that is printed.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2025-og-heidelberg-03": {"id": "model-2025-og-heidelberg-03", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "Results: Wet Lab Validation of Circuit Switch Behavior", "function": "limitation", "text": "The slight upward deviation in the fitted nH value could be due to experimental noise, minor cooperative effects not accounted for in the model, or simplifications in the model itself. However, these small discrepancies do not change the core conclusion: the system's dose-response curve is slow-switching. This level of agreement is sufficient for early-stage screening to identify and exclude designs with clearly undesirable characteristics and to prioritize architectures for more detailed in-vitro characterization.", "why_it_works": "A predicted Hill coefficient of 1.002 against a fitted 1.373 is explained by three candidate causes, one of them the model's own simplifications. Then the two sentences that keep the reader: the conclusion that survives the mismatch, and the standard of accuracy this model actually needs to be useful.", "teaches": ["uncertainty", "clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2025-ug-peking-03": {"id": "model-2025-ug-peking-03", "prize": "model", "team": "Peking", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/peking/model", "section": "Overall Structural Stability of Protein Complex", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "Radius of gyration (Rg) characteristics: Total Rg remained stable at 3.02±0.03 nm throughout 100 ns simulation with only 0.99% coefficient of variation, demonstrating good overall compactness without significant expansion or contraction. Axial Rg analysis showed:\nX-axis Rg: 1.5-2.8 nm, mean 2.1±0.17 nm, coefficient of variation 8.2%;", "why_it_works": "A mean, a standard deviation and a coefficient of variation for each axis, with the simulation length attached to the global figure. The spread is reported as a property of the trajectory instead of being hidden behind a single mean.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}}};
